+## 2026-08-06 — N3's guard was restart-hostile and killed the D1 probe; downgraded to a warning
+
+**Found by falsifying my own status report, not by a test.** The 2026-08-05 entry above recorded
+"D1 — `d1meas` job 2461421 queued, StartTime 2026-08-06T06:55:12". Checking the queue before
+committing that entry showed the job was not pending: it had started at **02:42:11 and finished in
+9 s**, SLURM state COMPLETED, exit 0:0. Both legs were dead on arrival:
+
+```
+### PARTHENON ERROR
+Condition: !(pin->DoesParameterExist(bn,"rho_unit_cgs") || ...)
+Message:   <dust> rho_unit_cgs / t_unit_cgs / T_unit_K are no longer read...
+File:      src/dust/dust.cpp   Line: 51
+```
+
+**Mechanism.** `GetOrAddReal` INJECTS its default into the `ParameterInput`, and Parthenon dumps
+the resulting parameter list into every restart file's `/Input` `File` attribute. The D1 restart
+`runs/deep_amr/run/parthenon.out2.00001.rhdf` (2026-08-04, written by a pre-N3 binary) therefore
+carries
+
+```
+<dust>
+  rho_unit_cgs  = 5.4670000000000005e-19 # Default value added at run time
+  t_unit_cgs    = 1476998822551          # Default value added at run time
+  T_unit_K      = 10.015000000000001     # Default value added at run time
+```
+
+so on restart the keys EXIST and a guard written against *decks* fires on a file no deck ever
+wrote. The scan that justified the hard-fail ("ZERO of 295 *.in files set these inside `<dust>`")
+was correct and still is — it simply cannot see a value the old binary injects at runtime.
+
+**Severity.** This is not one broken probe. It would have made **every restart written by a pre-N3
+binary with `<dust> evolve=true` unloadable by any post-N3 binary** — the flagship production chain
+included, the moment it steps forward a binary. That is the opposite of what an audit fix is for.
+
+**Fix** (`src/dust/dust.cpp`): the `PARTHENON_REQUIRE_THROWS` is now a rank-0 warning naming each
+dead key, the value the run actually uses, and the relative difference. This still satisfies the
+real goal — the deck cannot *quietly* claim a normalisation the code does not use — and makes dust
+consistent with `<radiation>`, which had already reached the same conclusion from the other
+direction (`radiation.cpp` ~line 95: 165 decks carried the dead keys, and hard-failing "would break
+decks belonging to queued jobs"). Same defect, same remedy, now the same treatment.
+
+**Blast radius checked, and it is bounded.** Both pending production jobs — 2449283 `root512sw`
+(`runs/root_ladder/submit_root.sh`, StartTime 10:52) and 2454638 `w1b150r7`
+(`runs/deep_amr/submit_wp1_bind150_r7.sh`, 14:53) — run `build_gpu_v4/bin/athenaPK` (869c1d34),
+which predates the guard: `strings build_gpu_v4/bin/athenaPK | grep -c "are no longer read"` = **0**,
+against **1** for `build_gpu_v5`. Only the D1 probe was pointed at v5. The queued chain was never
+exposed.
+
+`units/physical_units.hpp:105` carries a structurally identical `PARTHENON_REQUIRE` on
+`<units> rho_unit_cgs/v_unit_cgs/length_unit_cgs`, and it is **not** exposed: in the `have_be`
+branch those three keys are never `GetOrAdd`ed, so nothing injects them. Confirmed against the
+restart, whose `<units>` block holds only `mu_thermal` and `code_{length,mass,time}_cgs`. The same
+restart does carry explicit `<radiation> rho_unit_cgs/v_unit_cgs/length_unit_cgs` from its deck —
+already handled by the warning path.
+
+### Gate
+
+New deck `runs/audit_fix_regress/J_dust_restartkeys.in` = `H_dust_ideal.in` with the three keys
+written into `<dust>` at exactly the values a pre-N3 binary injects. Pre-fix it aborts at parse.
+Post-fix, on binary `d8ab5161`:
+
+- runs to completion, printing **3** `### WARNING Dust:` lines, one per key;
+- output **bit-identical to the `H_dust_ideal` baseline** — `hst 60794082…`, `00000 82c6627e…`,
+  `final e014cb33…` — which is the part that matters: it proves the keys are *ignored*, not
+  honoured. A warning that still let the value through would pass a "does it run" check and fail
+  this one.
+
+The warned relative differences independently reproduce the drift figure N3 was written against:
+rho **+3.149e-05**, T **−1.434e-05**, t **−3.24e-14** (the audit note's +0.0031 % / −0.0014 % /
+−0.0000 %).
+
+**Lesson worth keeping:** a `DoesParameterExist` guard cannot distinguish "the user asserted this"
+from "an older binary of mine wrote this into a restart". Guards on parameter *existence* are
+restart-hostile by construction; guard on a *disagreeing value*, or warn.
